 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/subsets-ii/",
 "pattern": "Backtracking + skip equal neighbours",
 "statement": "Given an array `nums` that **may contain duplicates**, return all possible subsets. The answer must not contain the same subset twice.",
 "example": {
  "input": "nums = [2,1,2]",
  "output": "[[],[1],[1,2],[1,2,2],[2],[2,2]]",
  "why": "Both 2s are interchangeable, so [1, 2] is listed once even though either 2 could be used."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "nums = [1,2,2]",
   "output": "[[],[1],[1,2],[1,2,2],[2],[2,2]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "nums = [0]",
   "output": "[[],[0]]"
  },
  {
   "input": "nums = [4,4,4]",
   "output": "[[],[4],[4,4],[4,4,4]]"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Start from the plain Subsets recipe: record the current choice, then try each later number, explore, undo.",
  "Duplicates break it: with two 2s, *picking the first 2* and *picking the second 2* for the same slot build identical subsets.",
  "Fix: **sort** so equal numbers are neighbours, and at each slot use a value only **once** — if `nums[i]` equals the number just before it (and that one was already tried for this slot), skip it."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def subsetsWithDup(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:\n        def backtrack(start, subset):\n            subsets.append(subset[:])  # Append a copy of the current subset\n\n            for i in range(start, len(nums)):\n                if i > start and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:\n                    continue  # Skip duplicates at the same depth level\n                subset.append(nums[i])\n                backtrack(i + 1, subset)\n                subset.pop()  # Backtrack\n\n        nums.sort()  # Sort the input to handle duplicates\n        subsets = []\n        backtrack(0, [])\n        return subsets",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n · 2ⁿ)",
  "timeWhy": "At most 2ⁿ subsets, each copied in O(n); sorting adds only O(n log n).",
  "space": "O(n)",
  "spaceWhy": "Recursion depth and `subset` are at most n (not counting the output)."
 },
